===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|yomi=k|え}}
<div style="float:right;">
{{swp|ja:}}
{{swp|Celtis sinensis}}
[[File:Celtis_sinensis%3DChinese_Hackberry.jpg|thumb|250px|{{m|ja|榎|tr=e, enoki}}: the '''[[Chinese hackberry]] [[tree]]'''.]]
</div>

From {{inh|ja|ojp|sort=え|-}}. Found as an independent noun in the ''{{w|Man'yōshū}}'', completed some time after 759 CE.<ref>{{RQ:Manyoshu|16|3872}}, text [https://web.archive.org/web/20220626150734/http://jti.lib.virginia.edu/japanese/manyoshu/Man16Yo.html#3872 here]</ref>

Some references<ref name="NJ">{{R:Nihon Jiten|id=46198}}</ref> suggest various theories regarding the ultimate derivation of this ''e''.  However, historical kana usage indicates different roots.
* From {{m|ja|枝||tr=e, eda|branch}}, from the many [[branch]]es.
* From {{m|ja|柄||tr=e|handle}}, from the way the wood was used for [[tool]] [[handle]]s.
** {{m|ja|柄|tr=e}} derives from {{m|ja|枝|tr=e}}, and both originate from an older pronunciation of {{IPAchar|/je/}} (''ye''), phonologically distinct from {{m|ja|榎|tr=e}}, which was just {{IPAchar|/e/}} (''e'') in the oldest sources. For example, {{m|ja|榎|tr=e}} and {{m|ja|枝|tr=ye}} were used in the [[w:Ametsuchi no Uta|Ametsuchi no Uta]], a [[pangram]] similar to the [[w:Iroha|Iroha]], written prior to the merger of {{IPAchar|/e/}} and {{IPAchar|/je/}}.
* From {{m|ja|餌||tr=e, esa|food for animals}}, from the multitude of berries eaten by birds.
** This originates from an older pronunciation of {{IPAchar|/we/}} (''we''), again phonologically distinct from {{m|ja|榎|tr=e}}.

Consequently, this {{m|ja|榎|tr=e}} appears to be a distinct and separate root, independent of these other terms.

